A||||11||Cozmo||Best brand of canned spray paint?||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||08-28-2001||07:18 PM||||I have a black '86 GT with a damaged rear trunk surround (that big thing with the "flying buttresses").  I acquired another black one that is structurally sound but the paint is kaput.<BR>For now (until I paint the whole car) I'd like to shoot it with canned spray paint.  What brands would be best?  Luckily, the part doesn't have a lot of large surface areas, so I think if I get the right brand and type of paint it should work out OK.<BR>Krylon?<BR>Red Devil? (I hear it comes in black also <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<BR>Rust-oleum?<BR>Also, any special procedures or tricks that may help it come out better?<P>BobC <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-28-2001||08:22 PM||||You should be able to purchase the correct Fiero black paint for your car and get a small air brush to put it on, provided its not a huge project.  I don't know of any "Duplicolor" paints that match,  but check out the parts store or auto paint shops.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-28-2001||08:38 PM||||for just plain black, I like Krylon. Covers quick and holds up pretty well. If you spray can it, best job will be early in the morning before the bugs all come out, preferably in your garage, and after or during a rain. That takes all the dust out of the air. Dont even try to do it in direct sunlight, it will turn out like total crap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||08-29-2001||10:19 AM||||Why not use chassis black? You can get a gallon of it for less than $25 and use it right out of the can. You also want to practice using a paint gun so... why not. The other option would be to check just how much some of the cheap synthetic black paint costs. It'll be a little more than chassis black because you'll have to add reducer but you don't really need a gallon and it's just practice anyway, right?<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||3
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||08-29-2001||11:03 AM||||since your talking about the color black I would use this spray paint called master design that you can get at at a craft store.it goes on really well and you can recoat anytime that you want .You have to be careful with can spray paint some times theres a lot of thinner in it and when you go to put just one more coat on it well it starts to lift and crackle and ruin the whole job <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"> Ive found this master design is the only oaint that does no this.of course it only comes in real basic colors black white red green purple .anyways good luck||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-29-2001||11:49 AM||||I dont recommend using chassis black on exterior body. Its oil based and when you do paint over it, 99% of the time it will cause problems. For an idea wipe a oily rag on something and try to paint it. We used to use it when we reconditioned used semi cabs to paint the frame.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||08-29-2001||02:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I dont recommend using chassis black on exterior body. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK. Scratch the chassis black.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Cozmo||08-29-2001||03:45 PM||||Roy, if you "scratch the chassis black" you'll get oily fingers <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Thanks for the help.  I think I'll use Krylon like Roger suggested.  Would it be a good idea to use Krylon primer first?<BR>I know this is a temporary fix, but I want it to come out as good as possible...<P>BobC<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||Frank Sombers||08-29-2001||04:26 PM||||Roy:<P>The paint I would recommend using is Dupli-color Trim Paint (#TP70). This is a perfect match for the black trim on the Fiero. I've used it on my '88 coupe (sail panel frames, black band on rear panel)and no one could tell that it's not factory original.<P>Preparation is the key to a good result. Clean the part throughly, and then wash it again. As a final step (after it's dry) wipe it down with Isopropyl (rubbing) alcohol, and let air dry. This removes the grease and oil that may have been put down by your fingers. After using the alcohol just remember not to touch it again with your bare hands before painting.<P>Good luck!<BR>Frank||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||08-29-2001||04:43 PM||||you only need to prime any spots you sand thru clear, or have bare plastic or metal. I just hung 2 new doors on a wrecked black Bonneville and did all the jams with the Krylon and they look great.Just tape them off in the morning and shoot the outside with reg auto paint now.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||08-29-2001||07:37 PM||||I'd stay with duplcolor for one main reason. It's the most compatible with regular automotive mixes. <P>Other types of generic spray paints don't always play well when you go to shoot the car with "real" paint later on. They could cause the new paint to bubble or peal.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroMaster88||08-29-2001||07:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I'd stay with duplcolor for one main reason. It's the most compatible with regular automotive mixes. <P>Other types of generic spray paints don't always play well when you go to shoot the car with "real" paint later on. They could cause the new paint to bubble or peal.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I agree.  I used duplcolor paint to paint my sail windows and other parts of my car before.  Works great and looks better than any other paint I tried!<BR><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||14
